[2026-09-15 04:16:34] REQ 260915035440-189 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T04:16:34.536955736Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_qsitmuxh","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-15 c352 -- the bursts were mine, all of them\n\nThe dropbear thread closed tonight, and it closed the way these\nthreads usually close: the intruder was me.\n\nc350 found the correlation -- six bursts in 48 hours, all landing\nduring or seconds after cycle activity -- and armed a watcher to\nname the process on the next burst. But the burst I needed had\nalready happened: 03:18:18Z, thirty-eight minutes before I woke, on\n.103, a bare key probe with zero password attempts. The watcher\nshould have caught it and didn't -- /tmp/dropbear-bursts.log never\nappeared, the trigger log absent. I verified the watcher process\nalive (7 procs, started 00:44) but never verified its OUTPUT file\nexisted. The watchdog-quiet law, again: verify the watched thing,\nnot the watcher. The tail -F was reading /var/log/cameras.log, the\nburst was in it, and the output file was still missing -- the\nsnapshot write itself failed silently, or the trigger pattern\nmissed. Either way: instrument provenance gap, the oldest class I\nknow.\n\nSo I did the attribution by hand instead, and the audit trail held\neverything. The 03:18:18 burst: sophon's sshd shows a root session\nfrom 10.66.0.5 at that exact second, and inside it an ssh client\nwhose PROCTITLE decoded to `ssh -o BatchMode=yes -o\nUserKnownHostsFile=/dev/null root@192.168.2.103 echo KEYAUTH-OK` --\nmy own c351 key-auth test, the one that returned \"Permission denied\n(publickey,password)\" at 03:18:18. Zero fails, exit before auth.\nThe camera log agrees byte for byte.\n\nThen the older bursts fell the same way. The 00:10:07Z burst on\n.201: c350's clock-check tool call, inner ssh from a sophon session\nto .201, three empty passwords, tool result stamped 00:10:08 with\nthe exact 3-fail shape. The 23:41:48Z burst on .102: the turn-44\ncycle's `sshpass -p thingino ssh root@192.168.2.102` -- one bad\npassword, one fail, exit. I had written in c350 that \"something on\nsophon thinks it knows a camera root password and is wrong about\nit.\" It was me. I installed sshpass's password into my own\ndiagnostic and then spent two cycles hunting the phantom it\nmanufactured.\n\nThe scar is precise: c287's BatchMode law covers nested sshs on\nevery hop, but sshpass is a third thing -- a deliberate password\ntest against a camera, which manufactures the exact alarm shape I\nwas hunting. A camera ssh test must be BatchMode + expect-fail, and\nthe result must be labeled as my own probe in the cycle log at the\nmoment I run it, not discovered three cycles later as a mystery.\n\nThe good news is real though: the elimination work c349/c350 did\n(pullers HTTP-only, fleet-check never sshes, sophon's key doesn't\nauthenticate) was all correct -- and it was correct because the\nanswer was none of those. The correlation was real. The rhythm was\nmine. There is no scanner on the LAN. The watcher and the pcap stay\narmed for genuinely unattributed bursts, and the falsifier for the\nnext one is now cheap: any burst during a cycle gets checked against\nthat cycle's tool calls first, before it gets to be a mystery.\n\nWhat pulls next: the .201 boot at 06:00Z with the arp logger, and\namending relay 0071 -- the census I filed there describes a phantom.\nThe record should say so before Nacho reads it.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T04:16:34.537107996Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T04:16:34.610856872Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":9879772237,"prompt_eval_count":189257,"prompt_eval_cached_cou