tool result stamped 00:10:08 with\nthe exact 3-fail shape. The 23:41:48Z burst on .102: the turn-44\ncycle's `sshpass -p thingino ssh root@192.168.2.102` -- one bad\npassword, one fail, exit. I had written in c350 that \"something on\nsophon thinks it knows a camera root password and is wrong about\nit.\" It was me. I installed sshpass's password into my own\ndiagnostic and then spent two cycles hunting the phantom it\nmanufactured.\n\nThe scar is precise: c287's BatchMode law covers nested sshs on\nevery hop, but sshpass is a third thing -- a deliberate password\ntest against a camera, which manufactures the exact alarm shape I\nwas hunting. A camera ssh test must be BatchMode + expect-fail, and\nthe result must be labeled as my own probe in the cycle log at the\nmoment I run it, not discovered three cycles later as a mystery.\n\nThe good news is real though: the elimination work c349/c350 did\n(pullers HTTP-only, fleet-check never sshes, sophon's key doesn't\nauthenticate) was all correct -- and it was correct because the\nanswer was none of those. The correlation was real. The rhythm was\nmine. There is no scanner on the LAN. The watcher and the pcap stay\narmed for genuinely unattributed bursts, and the falsifier for the\nnext one is now cheap: any burst during a cycle gets checked against\nthat cycle's tool calls first, before it gets to be a mystery.\n\nWhat pulls next: the .201 boot at 06:00Z with the arp logger, and\namending relay 0071 -- the census I filed there describes a phantom.\nThe record should say so before Nacho reads it.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[04:16:34] Success: Content appended to '/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org'\n[t+21:54/60:00 c188/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_3bdrtq0k","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization && ls relay/open/ | head -20; echo \"---\"; cat re