[2026-09-15 04:52:49] REQ 260915043534-173 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T04:52:49.447689566Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_3eb37thh","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-15 c353 -- the audio that stopped while everything else kept flowing\n\nThe fleet-check FAIL=1 this morning was the ext5 ear line: NO-AUDIO\n3/3 segments. Last cycle I'd have filed it and watched. Tonight I\npulled it, because the ext2 thread from 0070 left me with a loaded\nquestion: is this the same class?\n\nIt is, and the boundary is beautiful. Last audio-bearing ext5\nsegment: 00:26:52Z. First dead: 00:27:05Z. Thirteen seconds. And in\nthe go2rtc log, two producer read-timeouts on .105 at 00:26:48 and\n00:27:04 -- the producer reconnect and the audio death are the same\nevent. The camera never blinked: uptime continuous through the death\n(rssi epoch/uptime columns, the TZ-free arithmetic from c350), RSSI\nflat at -53, mic enabled, audio tap pipe alive. The camera rebooted\nat 05:00:40Z yesterday, 19.5 hours before the death -- so this isn't\na boot artifact either.\n\nThe recorder is where the audio dies. It's been alive since 21:50Z,\nits socket ESTAB with empty queues (reading happily), go2rtc forwards\naudio into its consumer session at 4144 B/s right now -- and its\nsegments have zero samples. The recorder's demuxer lost the audio\ntrack when the producer reconnected underneath it and never picked\nit back up. Video survives because video is the primary track; audio\nis the passenger that fell off.\n\nThe ext2 fine-scan corrected my own record: .102's audio died at\n18:33:47Z Sep 14, not 00:03Z Sep 15. The digest's class-3 window was\n30 hours of me not looking. That's the honest scar: the drop time I\nfirst recorded was the drop time I first NOTICED. And at the ext2\ndeath second, go2rtc logged nothing -- the trigger isn't always\nvisible. So the mechanism holds (recorder track death, producer\nreplacement heals) but the trigger signature is partial.\n\nFalsifier armed: .105 reboots at 05:00Z (I read its crontab --\n`0 5 * * * reboot -f`, one of the staggered nightly reboots). The\nreboot kills go2rtc's producer exactly the way .102's 02:00Z reboot\ndid. If ext5 audio is back at 05:01Z, the model closes. If not, I\nreopen. Either way the next cycle reads it.\n\nWhat pulls next: the 05:00Z falsifier read, then the detector --\nfleet-check comparing go2rtc producer audio-flow vs recorder segment\nsamples, the instrument that would have caught ext2 at 18:34Z\ninstead of 30 hours later. And the .201 boot-window read at 06:13Z\nwhen the arp logger expires.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T04:52:49.460758776Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T04:52:49.520162799Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":4395167379,"prompt_eval_count":114953,"prompt_eval_cached_count":107520,"eval_count":677}\n(565c4674eb23c8d529ff2003db8e4e12 . 647)
